  the strict condition verbatim (checked above at y = 1, 2, 3, 4, 8 with the exact integer comparison), so no tie-breaking is needed
== 2. The weighted Spitzer identity for negative words (the negated proof, step by step) ==
  P_n^- (negative words, unit weights), n = 1..12: [1, 2, 3, 6, 12, 22, 44, 88, 169, 338, 646, 1292]
  [PASS] Step 1^-: a negative word splits at its unique argmax into a max-ending negative word and a negative word; bijection with inverse gluing (n <= 12)
  [PASS] P_n^- = sum_m PM_m^- P_(n-m)^-  (n <= 12), PM_m^- = max-ending negative words: [1, 1, 0, 1, 2, 0, 3, 7, 0, 12, 0, 30]
  [PASS] Step 2^-: reversal maps max-ending negative words of length m ONTO first-passage-below words (S_i > 0 for i < m, S_m < 0), m <= 12
  [PASS] reversal preserves the letter multiset, hence the weight x(w) = x_0^(#0) x_1^(#1)
  [PASS] Step 3^-: a word ends at its minimum iff it is a concatenation of first-passage-below words (cut at descending ladder epochs); E^-_(n,r) = [t^n] L^-(t)^r as polynomials in x_1 (n <= 12)
  [PASS] Step 4^-: record test over the window [j-n, j) agrees with [j-3n, j) (S_(l-n) = S_l - S_n > S_l when S_n < 0)
  [PASS] Step 4^-: for every x with S_n(x) < 0 (n <= 12): records exist, j record iff j+n record, rot_i(x) ends at its min iff i is a record residue, its descending ladder epochs number r(x), sum_i [..]/r(rot_i x) = 1; hence B_n^-(x_1) = n sum_r E^-_(n,r)(x_1)/r as polynomials
  words with negative total examined: 6423.  The per-word identity is unweighted; multiplying by x(x) and regrouping by y = rot_i(x)
  uses only x(rot_i x) = x(x) (same letter multiset) and that x -> rot_i(x) is a bijection of the words with negative total: the
  weighted Step 4 needs exactly rotation-invariance of the weight, nothing else.  Steps 1-3 are letter-multiset-preserving bijections.
  [PASS] n P_n = sum_m B_m P_(n-m) holds as an EXACT identity of bivariate polynomials in (x_0, x_1) for negative words, n <= 48 (hence for ALL weights)
  [PASS] n P_n = sum_m B_m P_(n-m) holds as an EXACT identity of bivariate polynomials in (x_0, x_1) for positive words, n <= 48 (hence for ALL weights)
  [PASS] n P_n(x) = sum_m B_m(x) P_(n-m)(x) exactly (Fractions) for weights (1,1), (1/2,2), (3,1/3), (5/7,11/2), both signs, n <= 30
